  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES satellite imagery reveals a ridge extending into \nthe western Great Lakes reflecting a ~1020 mb sfc high planted atop \nthe state of Minnesota. Residual moisture in the wake of a departing \nshortwave has manifested into patchy dense fog and or stratus as \nnoted by area ASOS obs in N WI and the western UP. Fog is expected \nto lift through sunrise. Aforementioned sfc high meanders across the \nUP today through Tuesday, holding dry and seasonably warm weather in \nplace with highs pushing the upper 60s to the low and mid 70s south. \nPatchy dense fog may be possible again tonight.\n\nHeaded into the mid-week, west-southwest flow will overspread the \nregion as troughing moves into central Canada and the northern \nplains. Meanwhile, the trough over the Pacific Northwest will cut \noff over the four-corners region and track into the southern Plains. \nThis will bring increasing Pacific and Gulf moisture transport \nnortheast into the U.P. with PWATs reaching up to 1.75\" (95-99th \npercentile of climo) Tuesday night through Wednesday. With dewpoints \npushing 60F on Wednesday it will feel a bit humid for fall. There \nwill be multiple opportunities for precipitation Tuesday night \nthrough Wednesday night as warm, moist air advection increases along \nwith a 40-45kt low level jet providing a focus for large scale \nascent. Then a cold front will push through the area by Thursday \nmorning with additional showers preceding it and cooler, drier air \nbehind it. As far as rainfall totals ending early Thursday, there \nstill remains some uncertainty. The latest global ensembles have \ntrended the greatest QPF axis a hair south, highlighting a barely \n50% chance for QPF >0.5\" in the far southern UP (largely Menominee \nCo). Meanwhile the NBM and and LREF still favor a 50-70% chance for \nQPF >0.5\" across much of the central and southern half of the UP. \nThis is likely an influence of the Canadian ensemble, the obvious \noutlier, who favors the greatest QPF axis further north across \nessentially the entire UP. With deep moisture would expect that some \nlocations will overachieve with localized heavy downpours. \nRegardless, any rain is welcome as drought conditions have expanded \nacross the UP recently. Month-to-date percent of normal precipitation \nanalysis reveals the entire UP in a deficit between 10-50% of \nnormal, worst in the eastern half from Alger Co to Drummond Island. \nLuckily, no significant warm-ups or fire weather watch out patterns \npresent themselves in the forecast, unlike the warm and dry early \nOctobers of 2024 and 2025.\n\nExpect cooler, more seasonable temperatures to finish out the week \nand into next weekend with highs in the low 60s. Wouldn't rule out \nlake effect rain showers, especially over the eastern U.P. on \nThursday night into Friday morning as a passing shortwave brings the \npotential for deeper inversion heights and colder 850mb temps. The \nGEFS is the most aggressive among the ensembles with a mean of 0C at \n850mb while the LREF (EC/GEFS/CMC) mean sits around 3C with 0C \nfalling into the 10th percentile. With lake temperatures sitting \naround 13-16C would expect a high potential for lake effect \ncloudiness and a few light showers even with the warmer, mean \nsolution.",
